  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>From the figure, It is clear that diodes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are reverse biased, therefore they will not conduct any current and the resistance&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> will be not in use. The&nbsp;diode <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is&nbsp;forward biased, therefore the current flowing&nbsp;through the diode <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is same as the current through the battery.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/6de3e7c2-d986-4868-a896-ff5a78832d95-image.png\" style=\"width: 270px; height: 180px;\" /></p><p>On redrawing the&nbsp;circuit as&nbsp;shown, we can obtain the value of current as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
